  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 219 PM EST Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: GOES-19 mid lvl water vapor imagery indicates ridge and \nsubsidence is directly overhead this aftn while ribbons of better \nmid lvl moisture is angling toward the SLV/western NY. Meanwhile, \ndeep and closed off vertically stacked cyclonic circulation is over \nthe northern Plains with strong southerly warm-moist conveyor belt \nacrs the Ohio Valley. This deep moisture advection, combined with \ninitial pre-trof 700-500mb s/w energy wl result in a broken line of \nshowers on Thurs. Crnt proggs have highest probability of precip \nbtwn 12z-18z, with secondary line of precip possible btwn 21z-00z. \nDid note a weak sliver of MUCAPE of 150-300 J/kg, so a rumble or two \nof thunder is possible. However, given the weak thermodynamics and \nforcing confidence is low on thunder, so have not mention in grids \nattm. Rainfall amounts wl vary from one to location to another, \ngiven the scattered nature of showers, but with pw values 1.25 to \n1.40\" a few heavier downpours are possible. Basin average rainfall \nmainly btwn 0.10 and 0.25\" with highest values over the High Peaks \nin Essex County, NY. \n\nA very warm night is anticipated tonight with lows mid 40s to near \n60F, due to increasing clouds and southerly flow. Coolest values \ndeeper protected valleys of central/eastern VT and warmest CPV. As \ngradient strengthens btwn approaching cold front and departing ridge \nthe 925mb to 850mb wind fields increase btwn 25 and 45 knots tonight \ninto Thurs. The latest RRFS sounding profiles at BTV and CLCR show \n300 feet agl winds of 25 to 35 knots, but warmer air moving over \ncooler warm suggests a stable bl. Still believe with 33 knots at 300 \nfeet agl a Lake Wind Advisory wl be required tonight into Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Latest synoptic scale progs show additional deepening of \nmid/upper lvl cyclonic circulation over the central Great Lakes, \nwhile elongated vorticity and associated sfc boundary is draped acrs \nthe eastern seaboard. The system looks just a little unphased on \nFriday with our cwa underneath mid lvl dry slot. This should result \nin a mostly dry Friday with comfortable temps well into the 60s. As \nclosed circulation moves toward western NY/PA weak low pres develops \nover SNE coast with invert trof extending back toward central NY. \nThis complex mid/upper system and associated track of sfc low pres \nand placement/amount of precip on Friday night into Saturday is \nstill to be determined. The highest probabilities from the NBM are \nacrs central/eastern VT attm, but would not be surprised if chances \nexpand back toward northern NY, given the closed circulation. As \nsystem evolves on Saturday, expect developing cyclonic upslope flow \nto occur, placing highest pops/qpf acrs the northern Dacks into the \ncentral/northern Greens. Still some uncertainty on timing of \nupslope flow and when the cooler air aloft arrives. Temps wl be \nslowly stepping back toward normal by this upcoming weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Early next week is looking drier as large ridge of \nsurface high pressure builds over the eastern Conus. The \namplification and break down of this ridge area both handled \ndifferently between the GFS and ECMWF. The GFS brings the ridge in \nfaster and pushes it further north, while the ECMWF shunts it off to \nour south. For the second half of the week, the weather looks more \nactive as we will have upper level trough overhead and shortwaves \npassing through upper level flow bringing several chances for \nshowers.",
